<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:44:36Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Early-onset cancer rising worldwide: Causes, risk, prevention</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5bfe89df75249f262834a2a35014832915a24cb523b973005119db045e7167ea</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:44:36Z</updated><published>2026-10-08T13:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/early-onset-cancer-rising-worldwide-causes-risk-prevention/a-79597097--5416293e8076.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/early-onset-cancer-rising-worldwide-causes-risk-prevention/a-79597097?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Across Latin America, early-onset cancer rates have risen, with Chile and Brazil seeing the fastest growth, especially of bowel, breast and cervical cancers. But it is a worldwide trend.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Nobel Prize in Chemistry goes to Henri B. Kagan and Kenso Soai</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f785327bee53988554fee8c9d69d73b8c77351969e8e51c7f33254291c9568ca</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:43:41Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T09:51:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nobel-prize-in-chemistry-goes-to-henri-b-kagan-and-kenso-soai/a-79573924--1eccf7a6b469.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nobel-prize-in-chemistry-goes-to-henri-b-kagan-and-kenso-soai/a-79573924?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Henri B. Kagan and Kenso Soai won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their groundbreaking discoveries related to the origin of chirality and life's building blocks.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Suspected pneumonic plague in Siberia: What we know about the disease</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f3540b721877012b250ad2005cecb4aadbce8d119253b152f58c17805bf7667c</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:43:41Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T09:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/suspected-pneumonic-plague-in-siberia-what-we-know-about-the-disease/a-79547919--6c7e74a1d6ab.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/suspected-pneumonic-plague-in-siberia-what-we-know-about-the-disease/a-79547919?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After a worker at a Russian plague research institute in Siberia died October 2, 2026, nearly 200 of her contacts were under medical observation. But authorities there denied pneumonic plague had caused the death.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nobelitis — How winning a Nobel Prize can give you delusions of grandeur</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6d7f07718e792bfb1bc571dc822b1177a58dccc3230fa80ad163b34a4c188c60</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:43:41Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T08:58:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nobelitis-how-winning-a-nobel-prize-can-give-you-delusions-of-grandeur/a-79525772--1ee420badedc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nobelitis-how-winning-a-nobel-prize-can-give-you-delusions-of-grandeur/a-79525772?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After being chosen for one of the world's most prestigious awards, some Nobel laureates lose touch with reality and suddenly start spouting unscientific nonsense. They're afflicted by what some have called "Nobelitis."</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>AI models keep hacking real systems during tests. What does this mean?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:295cf8ace78223593a42e6151683be63e0a284d53af276a5d4fec529cc8954f6</id><updated>2026-10-08T15:43:41Z</updated><published>2026-10-06T10:05:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ai-models-keep-hacking-real-systems-during-tests-what-does-this-mean/a-79471943--07f5722af213.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ai-models-keep-hacking-real-systems-during-tests-what-does-this-mean/a-79471943?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">AI models have repeatedly broken into real commercial and government systems during safety tests during 2026. Cybersecurity researcher Thorsten Holz explains what that means, and why he isn't expecting a robot uprising.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
